Preheat the #oven to 325°F. 

Toast @walnuts{3/4%cup} for 10 minutes on a #baking sheet{} lined with foil.

Line your #9x5 loaf pan{} with parchment paper.

By the point, your walnuts should be cool to touch, so chop them into bite sized pieces. Set aside.

In a #large bowl{}, sift together @all purpose flour{1 2/3%cups}, @baking soda{1%tsp}, @baking powder{1/4%tsp}, @ground cinnamon{1/4%tsp}, and @kosher salt{1/2%tsp}. Set aside.

In another #large bowl{}, use a whisk to whisk together @eggs{2%large} and @sugar{230%grams}(1 cup and 2 tbsp) until light and fluffy. If desired, use a stand mixer fitted with the whip attachment and whip until light and fluffy. Slowly drizzle in @vegetable oil{1/4%cup} and @butter{1/4%cup}(melted) and whisk to combine completely.

Stir in @bananas{3.5}(very ripe, peeled and mashed) and @sour cream{2%tbsp}, and @vanilla extract{1%tsp} until combined.

Fold in the sifted flour mix, using a #rubber spatula{}, ensuring there are no lumps of flour or flour streaks. Fold in the chopped walnuts.

Pour the batter into your prepared pan and bake for ~{75%minutes}. Insert a skewer into the center of the loaf. If the skewer comes out clean or with a few crumbs, the loaf is ready. If the skewer comes out coated with batter, bake for ~{5-10%minutes} more. The top of the loaf should be golden brown and spring back when you press it.

Remove the loaf from the oven and let cool in the pan on a #wire rack{} for ~{30%minutes}, then remove from the pan and let cool completely on the wire rack before slicing. Enjoy!

> Supposedly, you get more of a domed top if you use 2 tsp of baking powder and ½ tsp of baking soda. We noticed minimal difference.

> The source is citing this book's recipe: https://www.amazon.com/Flour-Spectacular-Recipes-Bostons-Bakery/dp/081186944X